irectly applies the `GROUP BY` and aggregate functions to the data source referred to in the `FROM` clause of the inner query before the projection. The outer projection layer is eliminated if it only renames the columns without transforming them.\n\nThe rewritten logical plan shows that the original complex join condition is broken down into smaller joins between individual tables, and the aggregate functions are applied to the data source referred to in the `FROM` clause. This simplifies the query and makes it more efficient for execution."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 3:\n"""By analyzing the logical plan changes, we can see that Case 1 of the query rewrite rule applies. This is because some filter conditions are placed on the result set of an INNER JOIN operation, and these conditions do not reference columns from both tables involved in the join but can be logically applied to either input of the join.\n\nThe original query has multiple joins and filters, which can be optimized by applying the filter conditions at different levels. The rewritten query has a more optimized join order, with some filter conditions moved into the ON clause or as a WHERE clause on a subquery of the respective side.\n\nFor example, the filter condition `an.name IS NOT NULL` is moved from the WHERE clause to the LogicalFilter condition in the rewritten query. Similarly, the filter condition `(n.gender=\'m\' OR (n.gender = \'f\' AND n.name LIKE \'A%\'))` is also moved to a more optimized location.\n\nThe query rewrite rule applies these transformations to optimize the original query and improve its performance."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 4:\n"""The rule transforms `(x AND y) OR z` into `(x OR z) AND (y OR z)` and `(x OR y) AND z` into `(x AND z) OR (y AND z)`. In our case, we can apply this transformation to the `WHERE` clause. For example, the condition `(an.name IS NOT NULL AND (an.name LIKE \'%a%\' OR an.name LIKE \'A%\'))` can be transformed into `(an.name IS NOT NULL OR an.name LIKE \'A%\') AND (an.name LIKE \'%a%\')`. This transformation simplifies the `WHERE` clause and reduces the complexity of the query execution."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 5:\n"""The rule replaces IN with INTERSECT for querying intersecting datasets to potentially improve index usage and query speed. It also rewrites conditions using the OR operator into a series of UNION ALL operations to enhance code maintainability and performance. Additionally, it uses EXCEPT instead of NOT IN or anti-joins to minimize duplicate row processing and optimize resource use, effectively reducing execution time."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 6:\n"""The rule optimizes direct `IS NULL` queries on indexed columns by efficiently using the index. It also eliminates redundant `IS NULL` checks on columns defined as `NOT NULL` and simplifies the query logic."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 7:\n"""The rule simplifies constant expressions upfront by transforming `(a<b AND b=c) AND a=5` into `b>5 AND b=c AND a=5`. It also eliminates redundant or tautological conditions and converts nonconstant values in range conditions to constants, facilitating the use of indexes and improving query speed."""'}], 'response': 'Here is a summary of the provided SQL query rewrite strategies:\n\nThe given SQL query can be rewritten using various strategies. Query Rewrite 1 simplifies the WHERE clause by removing statically determined conditions and rewriting complex conditions. Query Rewrite 2 applies Case 1, breaking down joins into smaller joins and applying aggregate functions to simplify the query. Query Rewrite 3 optimizes the join order by moving filter conditions to more efficient locations. Query Rewrite 4 transforms AND-OR conditions in the WHERE clause for simplification. Query Rewrites 5, 6, and 7 optimize queries using various techniques such as replacing IN with INTERSECT, eliminating redundant IS NULL checks, and simplifying constant expressions upfront.', 'time': 1.5505924224853516}
